69
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Why don't you describe exactly what you were after in going through the work to write this book?

70
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:16,000
It must have taken ages.

71
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
It was a labor of love, Clark, but it actually literally took some years.

72
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:29,000
It took us about four years to put it all together.

73
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:37,000
The stories that are in the book are actual stories that we could take and mold and write about

74
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
how the adult leader coached and mentored and taught the young scout.

75
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
What do we call those in the book here? campfire yarns,

76
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Yarns, just as Maiden Powell called his stories yarns-

77
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
We adopt his terminology.

78
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
They are tremendously helpful in kind of putting some meat on the bones of the concepts that you're talking about,

79
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:03,000
because a lot of what was in them was very familiar to me as a scout master-

80
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Pretty typical stories that happen in scout troops all over the country.

81
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Actually, a lot of what scout masters will see- or I'm experienced scout masters will see- in our book.

82
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
As you say, Clark will be very familiar.

83
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:24,000
It will be situations that they've confronted themselves or that they've seen other leaders in their units confront.

84
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:32,000
One of our goals is to suggest at least what's the most appropriate or effective, or at least some ideas

85
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:41,000
for some good ways to respond or to react that will further the goal of leadership development.

86
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Scouting is a game of the purpose

87
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:53,000
In scout leadership for youth leadership. you have to turn it into a game for them

88
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:58,000
so that they don't really realize that they're actually learning something. but it's a game.

89
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:05,000
The yarns are a good way to start. Everybody likes a good story.

90
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
There's a lot that I've read in the book that I've written on the blog in years past.

91
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
We're all taking the lead from good old Baden Powell.

92
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:20,000
It's not unusual that we should have some of the same ideas and things, but it's been pretty amazing.

93
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:28,000
It's not, of course, that any of us are thinking that any of this is particularly unique.

94
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:31,000
As you say, this is what Baden Powell intended.

95
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Yeah, he was there before any of us.

96
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
He defined all this and it seems like that. at least some of us in the Scouting organization

97
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:47,000
have gotten away from some of the basic things that were probably very clearly evident in 1911..

98
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:54,000
It's just not as emphasized in the training materials and it's kind of there but it's not highlighted.

99
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:59,000
You know I teach- this is Harry, I teach Scoutmaster Specific-

100
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
And the piece for Patrol Method is: you know you get 20 minutes.

101
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:09,000
You just hit the highlights. You really can't teach the Scoutmasters everything that they really need to know.

102
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:16,000
You talk about leadership, training and mentoring as being something that is hands-on and not theoretical.

103
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,000
You take a young Scout. He's been in the classroom all day long. so when he comes to a Scout meeting

104
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
you don't want to sit down and talk leadership,

105
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
You want to put some of it into practical application.

106
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:37,000
You allow that 11,, 12,, 13-year-old boy that's in charge of his patrol, you know, kind of make a mistake,

107
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:43,000
and then you're pulling off to the side and you say, OK, what could you have done a little bit better,

108
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,000
What went right, What went wrong

109
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
And what can you do to improve on it the next time?

110
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:57,000
You let the boy kind of make those decisions, but you as the leader don't make them foreign.

111
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Harry, you're telling me that these guys are going to make mistakes-

112
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
You know, I don't know, but too many leaders that don't make mistakes every day.

113
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:11,000
We, as the leaders, have to be there to kind of pick them back up and say, OK, you made a mistake,

114
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
All right, so yeah, you got to be there to kind of coach them along and show them, maybe, a different direction.

115
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
You spend a lot of time in the book talking history.

116
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Why is it important that we understand the history of scouting?

117
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Baden Powell lived 100 years ago

118
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,000
And so you could look back and say, OK, well, yeah, scouting is traditional. of course

119
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
We've got all these great traditions.

120
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
What did what the Baden Powell wrote almost a century ago?

121
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:40,000
What does that really have to do with what's going on in the 21st century?

122
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:50,000
And the fact of the matter is that if you go back and read what Baden Powell went through in order to create scouting,

123
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
what influences did he have upon himself?

124
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
Where did this all come from?

125
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:00,000
What you end up finding is that he had a mixture of remarkable influences,

126
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And out of it, out of all of that, came what he referred to as his experimental summer camp at Brown Sea Island.

127
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
It wasn't just the first Boy Scout summer camp.

128
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:18,000
It was actually the model for the successful implementation of the patrol method.

129
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
That has never been done before.

130
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
I mean, he was the first to do that

131
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
And it was revolutionary.

132
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:30,000
If you want to be an effective leader, you got to know a little bit about scouting.

133
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:40,000
So you got to learn about Baden Powell and some of the other great leaders that we've had within the scouting movement.

134
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
We teach US history in our schools.

135
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Why is that?

136
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:50,000
So that we can understand why our forefathers wrote the Constitution and Declaration of Independence.

137
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:55,000
There's something about the history of scouting that helps put things in context for us.

138
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:02,000
And then I was very impressed with a statement that you make in the book about stewardship and the stewardship of the scouting movement.

139
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
What can you tell me about that?

140
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:17,000
Modern scout leaders are actually following in the footsteps of a century of highly illustrious, highly motivated, highly passionate and experienced scout leaders that went before us.

141
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:22,000
We are the ones who have now been handed the torch, we scout leaders,

142
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And we owe those in the program who came before us the responsibility to carry that torch as effectively as possible into this new century.

143
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Okay, so let me give you this scenario:

144
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:42,000
I'm a brand new, newly minted scout leader and you've got 30 seconds to talk to me.

145
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
What are you going to tell me to do?

146
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Read the book,

147
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Go talk to your SPL and ask the SPL what he wants you to do,

148
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
And hopefully the SPL will tell you to go sit in the corner.

149
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
If we need your help, we'll let you know

150
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:06,000
What we say on page 10 of the book. there's a paragraph in there which actually, arguably, tells it all.

151
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:13,000
It says leadership, training and mentoring in a scout troop is actually very simple.

152
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,000
Any scout master who puts his mind to it can do it.

153
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Trust and respect your scouts.

154
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
Use the patrol method.

155
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Give your scouts real responsibility. mentor, motivate, recognize results.

156
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
What we would tell a new scout leader is: do those things.

157
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
Those are the important things to do.

158
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Look at the program from the standpoint of the boys and do those things.

159
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:50,000
But in order to do those things right requires either experience, which obviously takes some time to get,

160
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:59,000
or you can also go and talk to your scout master- or district training people or other resources.

161
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:04,000
And we also quote another thing in this book that Will Rogers once said.

162
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:07,000
He said there are three kinds of men: the ones that learn by reading,

163
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:12,000
the few who learn by observation and the rest of us have to pee on the electric fence and find out for ourselves,

164
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And that's what happens to most scout masters.

165
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:23,000
It's a trial and error occupation, I guess you could say.

166
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:29,000
So what we'd like to do is to give some new leaders a shortcut, rather than having to learn everything the hard way.

167
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Maybe we can give them sort of a leg up on the learning curve as well as an orientation,

168
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:42,000
So instead of heading off in a direction other than the one that will lead them to a scout led and scout run troop,

169
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:49,000
we can sort of orient them in the right direction, that in their journey in that direction toward a scout led troop

170
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:59,000
that they will find a more rewarding and more effective leadership role for themselves.

171
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
In particular, they'll be much more effective in teaching their scouts how to become leaders.

172
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:12,000
So at one point you guys were both newly minted adult leaders and you jumped in with both feet.

173
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:19,000
What has changed about your approach from that first day to what you're doing now?

174
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:28,000
I think we can both say that radical changes have occurred since each of us began being scout leaders.

175
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:37,000
We are both very energetic, sort of potentially do-it-all kind of people who have ideas,

176
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
And I think that when we both started and had newly minted adult leaders,

177
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:50,000
we were not effective in terms of developing a scout led and scout run program.

178
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:54,000
We were acting as patrol leaders ourselves, as adult patrol leaders,

179
00:14:54,000 --> 00:15:01,000
as opposed to scout leaders who will allow the boys to be the patrol leaders and the boys to be the leaders.

180
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
Isn't it kind of messy when you let the boys do that?

181
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
That's the fun part.

182
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,000
That's the fun part.

183
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
I'm a retired command sergeant major after 30 years in the Army.

184
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:25,000
They say the big difference between the Army and Boy Scouts is that the Boy Scouts are adult leaders.

185
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Every time one of my non-commissioned officers made a mistake, if I crucified them they wouldn't learn,

186
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
They would shut down.

187
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
And I find the same thing with the boys.

188
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:41,000
The boys. if you always are telling them that this is what you did wrong,

189
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:45,000
what you did wrong, what you did wrong, well then they're going to stop doing anything

190
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Because all they know is I'm just going to get hollered at by Mr Wimbrough

191
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:54,000
Because it's not being done his way.

192
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:05,000
And that's where I had to step back and really take a look and kind of adjust my leadership style from the Army to the Boy Scouts.

193
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:10,000
And it works in both places.

194
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Yes,

195
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:16,000
It works in scouting and it worked in the Army too.

196
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:24,000
When I first became a non-commissioned officer at the age of 19, I was a pretty hard charge in NCO.

197
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
I had a wise squad leader of the tune sergeant, pulled me off to the side and said:

198
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
Sergeant Wimbrough says you're going to go far in this man's Army.

199
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:40,000
However, you need to change some of your leadership style.

200
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
So I did.

201
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:49,000
There's a particularly interesting section of the book, and section 7 is called Planning:

202
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
How to Facilitate Without Taking Over.

203
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:57,000
How do you facilitate youth planning without just kind of jumping in and doing it for them?

204
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Our experience, Clark, is that that actually is one of the most difficult things for a Scout Master to do.

205
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Scouts that are age 14,, 15, a troll leader age- are not generally very good at long-range planning.

206
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Their time window for planning is totally different from adults.

207
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Oftentimes Scouts are worrying about what they're going to have for lunch

208
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
Five minutes before they're supposed to eat it.

209
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
We're asking them to do some planning where. so it's October, next June.

210
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
we're going to go to, we're going to go caving next June and we better start making the arrangements.

211
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
And they'll look at you like you have to have your head examined.

212
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
That's next June. What are you talking about? Why are we worried about that?

213
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
That's a lifetime away.

214
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:48,000
If you're a program director for a camperee, you're planning that camperee a year out.

215
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:53,000
You know about it at troop meetings and the boys? just an example:

216
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
We had a camperee last weekend.

217
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
The boys started really planning for two weeks ago

218
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:05,000
And then the meeting before the camperee is. they finalized their meal plans

219
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:10,000
and figured out who was going to buy what food and what time they were going to meet.

220
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:16,000
They had it well under control. As adults we do long-term planning and short-term plan.

221
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:20,000
The boys plan: when am I going to eat? When am I going to sleep?

222
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
When am I going to eat? When am I going to sleep?

223
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:28,000
I have a pair of socks. that's good. I don't have to worry about anything else.

224
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
Have you been talking to my scouts, Harry?

225
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:36,000
I've been talking to my son.

226
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:44,000
We're going to go camping, but they don't think about the tour permits, safety forms

227
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
and all the health forms and stuff like that.

228
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,000
That's where you've got to kind of coach them along on that area

229
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:56,000
until when they start getting used to doing some of the planning.

230
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:03,000
then they can say, oh, we need to talk to somebody on the committee to get our tour permit.

231
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,000
We need to make sure that we have the permission forms.

232
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:13,000
But it takes time and it takes the coaching and the mentoring of an adult leader to be able to do that,

233
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:19,000
And it's actually a very valuable skill that an adult leader will be teaching his scouts

234
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
in taking them through that exercise.

235
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
It seems to us that there's some pretty important things involved.

236
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:28,000
One of them is the expectation of how much planning.

237
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:36,000
So adults have an expectation of probably much more intense and much more detailed planning

238
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:41,000
than any scout is going to have, unless maybe they're an older scout.

239
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Also, one of the points we make in our book is that, well, depending upon what it is,

240
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
you may not need that intense level of planning.

241
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:57,000
So, for example, if you're a type A scout master and you don't want to disappoint the parents

242
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
by making your unit look like it's disorganized,

243
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
you want everything organized and running like clockwork.

244
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
that means you need a very detailed plan

245
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:08,000
So that way everyone knows everything that's going to happen in advance.

246
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:12,000
Then our suggestion is: well, okay, look at what the objective is.

247
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:16,000
So if it's just a weekend camp out at a local park or whatever,

248
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
do you need that level of planning,

249
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
Or would you rather leave it up a little bit up in the air

250
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:27,000
and let the scouts work through it and then actually learn from the experience?

251
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
Sometimes you do need that level of planning

252
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
If you're going to be doing something pretty intense,

253
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:35,000
some kind of high adventure program where you are worried

254
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:38,000
about health and safety kind of issues. absolutely

255
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:43,000
The adult needs to make sure that those details are locked down and solid.

256
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:51,000
But it does seem to be helpful for a scout master to talk with the parents

257
00:20:51,000 --> 00:21:01,000
and to create an expectation that, look, this is not an adult-run and adult-planned unit. here

258
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:10,000
We give our scouts major responsibility for planning and execution

259
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:16,000
And if things don't go 100% perfectly, well guess what?

260
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
That's the way it's supposed to be

261
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:22,000
And as long as the boys have a good time, they probably planned it that way.

262
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,000
Well, I'm going to be the devil's advocate here for a moment.

263
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:30,000
I mean, honestly, guys, I can expect a 15,, 16,, 17-year-old scout

264
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:35,000
to be thinking about things like tour permits and permission slips and stuff like that.

265
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
I mean, I'm the scout master.

266
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:38,000
Isn't that my job?

267
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
Isn't that what I'm supposed to be doing?

268
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:43,000
So at that point it ends up being an issue of responsibility

269
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:49,000
And we agree, Clark, that it's going to be probably an undue expectation

270
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:57,000
for a 14,, 15-year-old scout to be worried about a tour permit

271
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:05,000
and whether all the medical forms are filed and are brought along with the unit,

272
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:09,000
But at the same time they shouldn't be oblivious to it either.

273
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
Maybe he's not getting the tour permit,

274
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:16,000
but he knows the adult on the committee that gets the tour permit for us

275
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:20,000
And that becomes a teaching point, that teaching and coaching.

276
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:26,000
you know you might want to say, hey, have you talked to the tour permit committee member?

277
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:31,000
So it becomes more of a teaching point than really for him to be applying.

278
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:34,000
But it's something foreign to think about as it goes on.

279
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:39,000
So maybe the next time when he does as a patrol leader, plans an adding,

280
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:44,000
he knows that. you know I need to talk to Mr Smith about tour permits.

281
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:48,000
I can see that, Harry, but, like I say, I'm still being a devil's advocate here.

282
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,000
That makes sense, but isn't that just as a scout master?

283
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
Isn't that just easier for me to do myself?

284
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
Yeah, it'd be easier for you to do it yourself,

285
00:22:56,000 --> 00:23:00,000
But what are you teaching the boy to do it yourself?

286
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,000
And actually, Clark, that's actually a great comment

287
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:06,000
because you could extend that to just about everything we're talking about.

288
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to do it himself?

289
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:12,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to define the whole plan for the whole year?

290
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Isn't it easier for the scout master to tell patrol leaders exactly what they're supposed to be doing?

291
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:21,000
Isn't it easier to just bypass the patrol leaders and, you know?

292
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:24,000
tell the scouts precisely what they're supposed to be doing?

293
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:28,000
That's not the point at all. It is easier.

294
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:32,000
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult run troop.

295
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
There is no doubt in my mind that that's the case.

296
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult run troop.

297
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Much less trouble, much less coordination, much more straightforward to have-

298
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
I'm sorry, to have an adult run troop,

299
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:48,000
A scout run troop. that's hard.

300
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:51,000
A scout run troop is harder because now the adult,

301
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:57,000
now the scout leaders have to start thinking about their interventions.

302
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:05,000
Do we really want to step in and command this or direct that or structure this,

303
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:09,000
Or is this something that the scouts are able to do,

304
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:13,000
perfectly capable of doing, and yet if we step in as adults,

305
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:17,000
the scouts will just back right off and let the adult do it.

306
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:22,000
I think most experienced scout masters would almost certainly say the same thing.

307
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:25,000
Oh yeah, adult run troops. that's easy,

308
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Much easier.

309
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:31,000
It's certainly a lot less wear and tear on the scout master's nerves,

310
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
Right, and it also takes less time.

311
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:36,000
The fact of the matter is that it takes more time to have a scout run troop,

312
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:41,000
like Wednesday, and actually Baden Powell specifically said that.

313
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
One of the things that he wrote- I can't quote it exactly,

314
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:46,000
but he said something up to paraphrase.

315
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:50,000
he said that what you're supposed to do is to allow your patrol leaders

316
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
to do as much as possible.

317
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:56,000
It's not so much to save the scout master time,

318
00:24:56,000 --> 00:25:00,000
it's to train the scout, and that's what the point is.

319
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:04,000
I continue in my advocacy role for the devil,

320
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:10,000
but parents in our troop are just not going to put up with me sitting around

321
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
and not doing these things.

322
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:16,000
My committee is going to go crazy if I tell them that we're going to let

323
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
the boys plan and lead these things.

324
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
They're expecting me to do that.

325
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
I mean, how do I handle a situation like that?

326
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:26,000
As the scout master, you have to tell them. you know, go sit in the corner,

327
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
Let the boys do it.

328
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:33,000
Let the boys come to you and ask you for what they need from the committee.

329
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:38,000
The committee is there to support. parents do more for their children today

330
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:42,000
than when you and I were growing up,

331
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:50,000
And so they have to allow their boys to spread their wings a little bit earlier

332
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:55,000
than what they normally would at the age of 18 or 18 or 19..

333
00:25:55,000 --> 00:26:00,000
And so you just got to pull them and say: this is a boy,

334
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
Let the boys do it.

335
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:07,000
Yes, it may look like chaos, but it's a boy controlled chaos.

336
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:10,000
The other thing that I would say is that I agree, Clark,

337
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:15,000
that your question is one of the biggest quandaries of a scout master

338
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:18,000
who wants to have a scout led troop.

339
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
Wait a minute, I'm the scout master.

340
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:23,000
All the parents are expecting me to be the leader.

341
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,000
All the scouts are expecting me to be the leader.

342
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
The program is expecting me to be the leader.

343
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
That means what?

344
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:34,000
Does that mean that I, as scout master, need to stand up in front,

345
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:38,000
make all the important decisions, or is there another approach

346
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:41,000
where it's the boys who are up in front?

347
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:44,000
It's the boys who are making the important decisions.

348
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:47,000
It's the boys who are the most visible leaders.

349
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:51,000
And I'll just quote from something which Ronald Reagan said.

350
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,000
we've got this quote in our book as well.

351
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:58,000
He says there's no limit to what a man can do or where he can go

352
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:01,000
If he doesn't mind who gets the credit.

353
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:05,000
The scouts are the ones who should be getting the credit for the successes

354
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:09,000
and taking the responsibilities for things that don't work out all that well.

355
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:19,000
So if I invest my scouts with the authority and the responsibility to plan these things,

356
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
aren't they going to miss some stuff?

357
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:27,000
I mean, wouldn't they get more out of it if I was planning outings and things?

358
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
Aren't they going to miss opportunity?

359
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,000
Just be the opposite.

360
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
Just the opposite.

361
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:40,000
If I, as the adult leader, plan everything, being the type of person that I am,

362
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:47,000
I've written a five paragraph operations order and have all my execution checklists

363
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,000
and execution matrix so that I know everything is going.

364
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:53,000
I've got to have this, this and everything is going well,

365
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
Yeah, okay, great.

366
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:02,000
But if the boys plan it and say they're going to learn from their mistakes,

367
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:07,000
they actually might miss some opportunities in one respect

368
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:11,000
and gain immeasurably other opportunities on the other side.

369
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:17,000
So in a world where our scouts- at least many of them in many parts of the countries-

370
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:22,000
are expecting to be entertained by adults and programmed up the wazoo,

371
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:27,000
out the wazoo, by the adults to put the scouts in charge

372
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
and have responsibility for figuring out what they're going to do.

373
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
That's an opportunity.

374
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:39,000
If I'm not making sure that it's really a whizbang program and everything,

375
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
aren't they going to just leave the troop?

376
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:47,000
How am I going to attract new scouts? if the scouts are just caught up

377
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
and leading and doing all this stuff all the time,

378
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
I mean they won't be doing anything fun.

379
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:52,000
That's the key.

380
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:55,000
They are doing fun things because they're the ones that are planning

381
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
the program with the resources that you've given them,

382
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:04,000
With the boys planning the program, with the boys executing their program.

383
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
that's what's going to keep them active.

384
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:09,000
Because if you, as the adult planet, they're saying,

385
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:12,000
huh, okay, yep, here we go again.

386
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:16,000
We're doing the same stuff and we're not doing what we want to do.

387
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:19,000
And the other thing I would add is that it's not like the scout

388
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:22,000
master just sits back and lets the program fall apart.

389
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:26,000
You might need to do that on occasion in order to make sure the boys

390
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:29,000
understand that it's their responsibility, that if they don't do it,

391
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:30,000
it's not happening.

392
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:35,000
But this is where the mentoring and the coaching and the advising comes in.

393
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
where you don't tell them what to do,

394
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:43,000
You do what any respectful leader will do when training a new leader.

395
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:44,000
Okay,

396
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
Let me show you some resources.

397
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
Let your scout develop it,

398
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:51,000
And at that point you're not the one who's dictating it,

399
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:52,000
He's deciding it.

400
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
He's got responsibility for it.

401
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:55,000
He'll execute it.

402
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:56,000
It'll be great.

403
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
He'll take the credit for it.

404
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,000
He'll have a big smile on his face,

405
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:05,000
He'll have learned a ton of stuff about himself and about how to be an effective leader.

406
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:12,000
So you guys must have a troop of just who are some kind of superhero scouts,

407
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:17,000
because my scouts, they wouldn't be able to do this, would they?

408
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:21,000
You know, any scouts would be able to do it.

409
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:25,000
And no, our own troop is not superheroes or anything like that.

410
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:29,000
So obviously it doesn't always work 100%,

411
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:36,000
Because basically these scouts who are planning these program events are inexperienced

412
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
and they have a lot to learn,

413
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:45,000
And so in the course of learning, they may not be able to put on something that is as perfect

414
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:50,000
and clockwork as an experienced Type A adult

415
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:53,000
who is planning the whole thing from beginning to end in thought of every detail.

416
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
It's just not going to be like that.

417
00:30:55,000 --> 00:31:01,000
It's more going to be like making sausage as opposed to having some pristine, elegant finished product

418
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,000
that's just handed off to the scouts and say,

419
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
okay, you guys execute this.

420
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:09,000
If you do this, it'll be great,

421
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:12,000
But you are sacrificing something.

422
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,000
There's no doubt about it.

423
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:24,000
In a scout run troop, at least until you end up being able to retain the 16 and 17-year-olds

424
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:32,000
who are now in the habit of taking responsibility and making these kind of decisions,

425
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:34,000
There's going to be a lag,

426
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:37,000
And this is where persistence comes in.

427
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:41,000
This is where you have to keep your eye on the objective. as a Scoutmaster.

428
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:43,000
What are you trying to accomplish here?

429
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:46,000
Are you trying to have a 100% perfect program

430
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:51,000
Or are you trying to train new leaders?

431
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:56,000
So I've been to the training and I watched the videos and everything looks really perfect in those videos.

432
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:01,000
And I showed this to my Scouts and I went and I worked with them on this

433
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:03,000
And they're looking at it like a dog with a new pen.

434
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:05,000
They don't seem to understand any of it.

435
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:07,000
They don't plan anything,

436
00:32:07,000 --> 00:32:09,000
They don't do anything.

437
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:11,000
So what am I supposed to do?

438
00:32:11,000 --> 00:32:14,000
Yeah, I mean, that's a tough one.

439
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:22,000
Well, yeah, but maybe what you have to do is take a boy that's not a patrol leader yet,

440
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:25,000
but is maybe a first-class Scout.

441
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:27,000
He wants to be a patrol leader.

442
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:34,000
So you're sending off to the NYLT, the National Youth Leadership Force.

443
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:38,000
That won't kind of get him excited about the program

444
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:46,000
And he brings it back in kind of making like a leadership trainer

445
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:52,000
so that he can an advisor to other patrol leaders, kind of like a troop guide for leadership.

446
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:57,000
If the boys just want to sit around and let the adults do it,

447
00:32:57,000 --> 00:33:02,000
well then what you do is you plan a weekend to where you're just going to sit around

448
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:05,000
And when the boys say, well, this wasn't very much fun,

449
00:33:05,000 --> 00:33:09,000
Then you say, okay, if you had a plan something

450
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:15,000
we might have been able to have some fun activities while we were on this camping trip.

451
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:17,000
But she left it all up to me as the adult.

452
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:25,000
The only thing I wanted to do was go out to the woods and sit on the rock next to the fire.

453
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:29,000
For me that's a fun time.

454
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:35,000
But maybe before that you have a conversation with your patrol leaders council

455
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:39,000
Say: gentlemen, this is your weekend,

456
00:33:39,000 --> 00:33:41,000
You're going to plan this weekend

457
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:47,000
And whatever you plan within reason,

458
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:50,000
as long as there's no issues with health and safety,

459
00:33:50,000 --> 00:33:52,000
ISA, Scout Master are going to go along with it.

460
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:55,000
But it's your responsibility to plan.

461
00:33:55,000 --> 00:34:02,000
And so you challenge them and make them understand that maybe for the first time in their whole lives,

462
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:05,000
they're able to actually plan their own time for a whole weekend.

463
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
They get to do what they want to do.

464
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:10,000
It's their responsibility and it's their privilege to do it,

465
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:13,000
And no adult is going to tell them: oh, you can't do that,

466
00:34:13,000 --> 00:34:15,000
You can't do this,

467
00:34:15,000 --> 00:34:18,000
You have to do this and you have to do that.

468
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:26,000
And that's an amazing opportunity for a 21st century- you know, 15th-

469
00:34:26,000 --> 00:34:29,000
Because most of them, many of them, at least in many parts of the country,

470
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:31,000
many of them, never get that opportunity.

471
00:34:31,000 --> 00:34:34,000
If I do this, if I tell them that they can do anything they want to do,

472
00:34:34,000 --> 00:34:39,000
all they're going to do is sit around and play video games and you know

473
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:44,000
they'll just eat whatever they want to eat and they probably won't get any requirements done,

474
00:34:44,000 --> 00:34:49,000
And you know. so how in the world can I just let them have carte blanche

475
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:51,000
to go and do whatever they want to do?

476
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:53,000
You just go and do requirements for the weekend.

477
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:56,000
Well, that's not the right way.

478
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:59,000
You go and you do an event and then you look at your,

479
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:02,000
have a look at their book and you see what requirements that they might have did.

480
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:07,000
And the other thing is that if you challenge your scouts to come up with something,

481
00:35:07,000 --> 00:35:08,000
you'll be amazed.

482
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:09,000
Exactly,

483
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:10,000
You'll be amazed.

484
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:14,000
Sometimes, often actually, they will come up with something

485
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:18,000
that you as a Scoutmaster would never have thought of in a million years.

486
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:20,000
It's not because it's not appropriate,

487
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:22,000
It's not because it's not safe.

488
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:26,000
It's entirely safe and it's entirely appropriate.

489
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:27,000
And it's theirs.

490
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:28,000
It's not your idea,

491
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:29,000
It's their idea,

492
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:35,000
And it might not be something that you as a- you know

493
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:39,000
as a 40 or 50 or whatever your old Scoutmaster- might think

494
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:42,000
is what those boys are going to want to do.

495
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:44,000
So if you had been dictating it,

496
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:47,000
you would never in a million years have thought of doing that.

497
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:49,000
But it ends up being a blast for them.

498
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,000
They absolutely love it and they own it.

499
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,000
It's theirs and they own it.

500
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:58,000
We're not wired the same way as a 14, 15-year-old is.

501
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:01,000
They're wired just a little bit different

502
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:06,000
And sometimes it's hard to remember that we were that 13, 14-year-old boy

503
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:09,000
doing the same thing.

504
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:14,000
Talk to me a little bit about you, have a section about caring leadership.

505
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:16,000
What is caring leadership?

506
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:18,000
Tell leaders three things.

507
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:20,000
One: lead from the front,

508
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:23,000
Set the example and take care of your people.

509
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:25,000
You've got to know them.

510
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:29,000
Everybody that's in your patrol is a valuable member,

511
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:35,000
But you as a leader, you have to know what's his likes and dislikes,

512
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:41,000
And caring leadership actually is one of the most important things

513
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:44,000
to get across to our young leaders,

514
00:36:44,000 --> 00:36:48,000
because it really is part of the key to the whole thing.

515
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:51,000
Anyone who's studied and worked with adolescents,

516
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:54,000
anyone who's tried to have anything to do with the Scout Troop, knows

517
00:36:54,000 --> 00:37:00,000
that a 14- or 15-year-old boy is pretty self-absorbed.

518
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:05,000
They're thinking. oftentimes they're thinking about one person and that's themselves.

519
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:09,000
To get them to think about the 11-year-old

520
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:14,000
who's just joined the Troop and needs some mentoring and some help.

521
00:37:14,000 --> 00:37:17,000
that's important.

522
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:23,000
Once again, that's something that at least some Scouts

523
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:27,000
is going to be a real revelation for them

524
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:30,000
that they're supposed to be thinking about younger Scouts.

525
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:33,000
They're supposed to be caring about those younger kids.

526
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:35,000
but that's what our program is based on.

527
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:41,000
I think, fellas, that we've just scratched the surface of what's in the book

528
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:45,000
and it's arranged in a way that's very accessible.

529
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:49,000
I think it's just an excellent resource for Scout leaders.

